  "query_preview": "..The mid-level trough that brought the rain and snow showers\nto eastern Arizona this morning has pushed off to the east, \nleaving clear to mostly clear skies, with light northerly winds \nand a fairly dry and cool air mass. The clear skies and light\nwinds will allow min temps to drop tonight, likely being some of \nthe colds temps so far this fall season for several places.\n\nRidging is expected to develop over the region by Sunday, allowing\nfor slightly warmer afternoon temperatures, along with some high\nclouds and winds becoming southerly but remaining light. Through\nthe end of the week an overall dry pattern is expected, but a\ncouple of weak troughs will move moving through the region. \nthe first weak trough is expected to pass north of Arizona Sunday\nnight or Monday morning, increasing mid to upper-level clouds. \nThis increase cloud cover will help to keep min temps slightly \nwarmer Sunday night, but due to the northern track of the trough \nand the lack of moisture, there is only a slight chance for \nshowers in far north to northwest Arizona. Dry weather then \ncontinues through Tuesday as another ridge moves over Arizona,\nfollowed by the next trough around Wednesday or Wednesday night. \nThe trough is expected move slightly further south compared to \nthe Sunday night-Monday trough, but the GFS, ECMWF and their\nensembles continue to depict little precipitation with this \nsystem, due to the lack of any notable transport of moisture into \nthe region as winds remain light. We will continue to monitor for \nany changes with the trough around Wednesday, but for now a drier \nand quiet weather pattern is expected into Thanksgiving. \nThanksgiving and Friday are looking dry at this time, with high \ntemperatures slightly above normal if this pattern holds.",
